WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.223
Expert performance is domain-specific.

2
00:00:02.223 --> 00:00:13.780
The conditions that define strong performance in technical work are not identical to the conditions in operations, incident response, leadership, communication, creative production, or physical skill.

3
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:19.559
Each domain has its own cues, constraints, risks, standards, and patterns of failure.

4
00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:27.116
A person can be excellent in one environment and still need practice, context, or support in another.

5
00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:32.006
Real-world conditions matter because performance is never separate from context.

6
00:00:32.006 --> 00:00:35.562
Time pressure changes how much analysis is possible.

7
00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:38.230
Incomplete information forces judgment under uncertainty.

8
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.563
Interruptions break attention.

9
00:00:39.563 --> 00:00:43.119
Tradeoffs require deciding what matters most right now.

10
00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:53.788
A clean classroom example may reveal understanding, but it does not fully prove performance in the environment where the skill will actually be used.

11
00:00:53.788 --> 00:00:57.789
This is why performance should be evaluated in context.

12
00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:04.457
A strong incident responder needs to prioritize, communicate, and update assumptions during an active event.

13
00:01:04.457 --> 00:01:11.125
A strong engineer needs to make sound decisions within architecture, cost, maintainability, and risk constraints.

14
00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:16.459
A strong leader needs to create clarity when people are under pressure.

15
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:23.127
The performance standard has to reflect the actual job, not an idealized version of it.

16
00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:26.683
Training and assessment should therefore include realistic constraints.

17
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:31.128
That does not mean every exercise must be high stakes.

18
00:01:31.128 --> 00:01:40.908
It means practice should gradually include the conditions that shape real performance: limited time, partial evidence, handoffs, interruptions, changing priorities, and consequences.

19
00:01:40.908 --> 00:01:49.354
The closer the practice gets to meaningful conditions, the more likely the skill will transfer back to the work.
